      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
          "content": "Scapho-trapezial-trapezoidal (STT) arthrodesis and proximal row carpectomy (PRC) are used for the treatment of Lichtman stage IIIB Kienböck's disease. This study prospectively compares 1-year results of STT arthrodesis and PRC in Lichtman stage IIIB Kienböck's disease."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
          "content": "Nineteen patients were operated: eight with STT arthrodesis and 11 with PRC. Preoperatively and 1-year postoperatively, mobility and grip strength were examined. Both DASH and Mayo Wrist Scores were obtained from the patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "In the STT arthrodesis group, mean extension/flexion worsened from 54 to 39 % of the opposite hand. Grip strength improved from 52.9 to 62.1 %. The DASH Score improved from 32.6 to 21.4, and the Mayo Wrist Score from 50.6 to 57.9. In the PRC group, extension/flexion decreased from 62.5 to 57.0 % of the opposite hand. Grip strength improved from 38.6 to 69.0 %, the DASH Score from 36.7 to 18.9, and the Mayo Wrist Score from 54.6 to 66.0."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "One year after operation, slightly better results were observed in patients with PRC compared to STT arthrodesis."
        }
      ]},
